well we begin in sudan where right now  thousands of people have taken to the  streets to protest against monday's  military coup major roads in sudan's  capital though have been closed and a  cyber security watchdog says the  internet has also been blocked the u.s  and the u.n meanwhile are calling on  sudan's military not to interfere with  these demonstrations at least 11 people  have been killed during protests just  this week  activists have been broadcasting  messages and mosques urging people to  continue their opposition to the  military's takeover  well how did we get to this point in  sudan the military seized power from a  transitional government led by prime  minister abdullah on monday  general abdul fatter al-berhan has  dissolved the sovereign council that  ruling body was established after omar  omar al-bashir was overthrown two years  ago now  brahan's also announced a state of  emergency he promises to hold elections  in july 2023 and then hand power over to  the elected civilian government still  protests have been held around sudan to  reject the return of military rule  well let's speak to our correspondent  heber morgan she's in khartoum for us  hereby no internet access is patchy to  say the least but what are you hearing  from the streets at the moment  well phone lines have been restored and  it's not clear that that is temporary or  it will be permanent it's been  on and off since monday since the coup  started but we're hearing from activists  on the ground that protests have started  in several locations around the capital  khartoum including khartoum's twin city  of omdurman people are taking to the  streets chanting that they don't want to  go back to a military rule and this is  despite the internet shutdown that has  been imposed since monday now general  abdel fattah al-burhan on the day he  made the announcement of the coup on  monday announced that he will be forming  a civilian transition government besides  the one that has already been dissolved  and that the power sharing agreement  between the forces of freedom and change  coalition and the military is still  valid with the exception of a few  crucial points and that it's uh he's  cancelling the article that says that  the transitional military council which  took over following the ousting of  bashir will be restored so people are  out in the streets saying that they  don't want the military to be in charge  they don't want the transitional  military council running them even if  there's a civilian transitional  government formed even if they're all  technocrats as he had promised there's  also concern for violence nostalgia or  10 protesters have been killed more than  140 have been reportedly injured since  monday's protest uh since protests  started on monday and their concerns  because of the widespread of security  forces on various streets and the block  the closure of bridges and major roads  and the heavy presence of security that  there will be violence between  protesters and security forces so  there's concerns that there will be  death tolls by the end of the day and  that protesters will face violence many  of them say that they're going to try to  try to stick to peaceful protests to get  their message across that they don't  want the army to leave them during this  transitional period yeah but you say  there are these concerns about violence  these are obviously planned protests the  military as you say has been preparing  can you talk us through a little more  about what they've been putting in place  obviously the eyes of the world are on  them  yes indeed so you could see tanks on  many of the bridges that have been  closed by the military leading to the  capital horton now yesterday they  announced that they will be closing all  bridges with the exception of two but  we've been seeing this morning lots of  spreads of us of military pickups  military trucks uh fully mounted with  soldiers and machine guns on the bridges  many of them are spread across major  checkpoints or major intersections  around the capital the road leading to  the army headquarters that has been uh  off limits since the 21st of october  when more pro when there were other  protests um with people demanding that  the military does not dissolve the  government this means we have to go a  few steps back nastasia before this all  started there was a  pro-military sitting in front of the  presidential palace demanding that the  military dissolve the transitional  government and there was a pro-democracy  sitting where tens of thousands also  took to the streets demanding the  military hand over power to civilians  and this was at the height of tensions  between the two sides so there is  concern that the security because of the  widespread security forces around the  capital blocking major roads you know  putting uh trucks with machine guns and  soldiers on board there's concerns that  the protesters and the the and the  soldiers will face each other and  several uh uh international actors have  called on the military to try to  maintain the peace and maintain uh the  peacefulness of this protest that is  expected to be very wide today but it's  not clear how the day will play out  we're going to have to just wait and see  how the military will handle the  protesters who are demanding that they  hand over power to a civilian rule  but as you say ahead of this crew we saw  some breakaway factions from the  civilian protest movement and they were  ostensibly actually being sponsored by  the military just how united is the  protest movement that we're seeing out  on the streets right now  well the breakaway movement uh was from  the force of freedom and change  coalition that is the coalition that led  the anti-government protests in december  2018. now they have accused the central  committee of that faction of that  coalition of being  exclusive and excluding many actors that  that were participating in the protest  against president obama bashir now when  it comes to when you look at the two  sides of the forces of freedom and  change coalition the central committee  and when you look at the forces of  freedom and change coalition the  national na the national charter  alliance as they call themselves they're  both on opposite side one of them is  supporting the military saying that this  is not a coup but rather it's a  correction of the path of the revolution  and the others are calling it a military  takeover a coup because of the  dissolution of the transitional uh  executive cabinet and the sovereignty  council when you look at how united the  central committee of the force of  freedom and change are they were able to  mobilize protesters without internet  with poor  with poor phone lines and communication  so it's it's clear that they still have  the power of the streets despite the  fact that over the past two years  economic progress has been very slow  reforms that people have been demanding  for years uh since the transitional  government took uh over has been very  slow as well but people say that they  understand that because this is a  transitional government there's very  little that they can do and they have to  wait until elections so  it's it's it's a battle of who has more  support in the streets and who can have  a stronger heart  stronger cards when they come to the  negotiating table should there be  negotiations there's been talks of  mediation efforts by the united nations  and by several countries and it's clear  that they're trying to mobilize supports  from the streets as much as they can  should they ever come to the negotiating  table and talk about what comes next for  sudan's transition and we are seeing  those crowds there build on the streets  in khartoum and andermann for now though  we'll be speaking to hibbert throughout  the day thank you hibba let's now bring  in jonas horner he is the deputy  director for the horn of africa project  and a senior analyst for sudan at the  international crisis group he joins us  now from nairobi join us as we've been  saying these are unsurprisingly some  fairly huge crowds and the military  response is obviously not going to go  unnoticed now given the internet  throttling and the road closures we've  been seeing what hiber was describing  there about the presence on the streets  how concerned are you that they're going  to actually crack down fairly hard today  i unfortunately think there's a strong  prospect of that  there have been significant and pointed  warnings coming from international  international actors both in sudan and  the diplomatic community outside of  sudan you know hoping to foresole any  violence that might come about today but  um you know the army has shown its face  on the streets in key centers of hartoum  at least today so far and so we're  really concerned that this may be um the  start of a new phase of violence in all  of this and i i think the other  component that we worry about is a  splintering of the military there's a  vast array of armed actors that are out  on the streets of cartoon and they all  will take uh their orders from different  centers uh and and that in itself holds  plenty of opportunity for  um for for much more chaos and violence  well this coup was really only possible  jonas because burhan and himeti the very  notorious general managed to make peace  with each other i mean he has quite the  reputation for violent crackdowns you  allude there to a potential  split within the military side how  united are they especially with  potential concessions um on the  horizon the  alliance that brought himety and berhan  together or back together really um you  know was was after a moment in which  they were almost at dagger's drone in  early june um but i think the current  agreement that has brought them into  a bit of a coalition is very much short  term in in outlook it doesn't account  for the clear and existential concerns  that exist in sudan that have existed  incidents since independence that really  is pit center and periphery  and really you know those who back  borhan those who back hameti have vastly  different concerns vastly different  interests at the end of the day so this  really is something of a short-term  connection for hermeti and bohan you  mentioned there some of the  international pressure obviously that's  been building on berhan and the military  side and i see he's now saying that a  new government will be led by a  technocrat and there are obviously  plenty of rumors going around but there  are talks also happening behind the  scenes  how likely is it that hamdock could  potentially return to power do you think  uh it's not out of the realm of  possibility hambuck has certainly done  his reputation which had itself been  battered in the last year or two but  he's done his reputation no harm by  taking such a principled stand in not  joining burhan's government in refusing  to dissolve the cabinet uh in the days  before this coup actually occurred and  you have a rather more clear set of  demands that have just come out from a  fairly cohesive set of protesters on the  streets and they're very keen to see  first of all the overthrow and removal  of burhan and hemetian and their arrest  they want to see the end of the state of  emergency and they do specifically seek  the restoration of handok at the head of  the transitional government i want to  ask you a little more about the timing  of all that we're seeing jonas because  obviously there was supposed to be a  transition from the military leading the  council to the civilians leading the  council and that by some estimates was  supposed to happen in november so i mean  the timing is obviously very interesting  when we saw the coup  now given that we're seeing people out  on the streets ostensibly rejecting what  the military is offering where does that  actually leave any kind of transition  well that really is that devil will be  in the details of the sorts of  negotiations that are going on um you  know particularly those currently that  are that are featuring international  actors i think the shame though in many  ways has been you know from the  beginning that there has been  insufficient inclusion of a broader  swathe of of sudanese to provide their  inputs to to offer a sense of what they  would like to see  in both the transition and then in sudan  going forward uh and i think any um  opportunity to incorporate those  interests those voices from the street  those people who are out there uh today  will be out there today uh it is key uh  i think the  form and and main uh stream of  negotiation has yet to really form but i  know the un is working hard on this uh i  know other external actors are working  hard on this and i think you know most  importantly of all there's a lot of  engagement between washington and the  gulf  that is probably the locus of power as  things stand as the internationals try  to support a return to a civilian-led  transition  and those voices on the streets they're  certainly very loud and plentiful today  jonas horner the deputy director for the  horn of africa project and the senior  analyst for sudan at international  crisis group always great to get your  thoughts thanks for joining us jonas  thanks mr

